{% sec 1, "Lending for a Living", "What is credit, and how do credit funds make money?", "Lending for a living" %}
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Lend £100 to a friend who always pays you back and you might ask for nothing extra. Lend it to a friend who sometimes forgets and you'd want a bit more. Lend it to someone who has already let you down twice and you'd want a lot more, or you wouldn't lend at all. That extra is the heart of credit.</p>
    <p>A company's bond pays a higher yield than a government bond of the same length. The gap is the {% gl "credit spread" %}credit spread{% endgl %}: the reward for the risk the company doesn't pay. It covers two things:</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>The chance of {% gl "default" %}default{% endgl %}</strong>: the company failing to pay interest or repay the loan.</li>
      <li><strong>How much you'd lose if it did.</strong> Lenders rarely lose everything; they usually get some of their money back, the {% gl "recovery rate" %}recovery rate{% endgl %}. The loss is what's left.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Multiply the two and you have the <strong>expected loss</strong>. A bond with a \(2\%\) yearly chance of default and \(40\%\) recovery expects to lose \(2\% \times 60\% = 1.2\%\) a year. If its spread is \(4\%\), the investor is paid well for that risk; if it's \(1\%\), she isn't.</p>
    <p>{% gl "credit rating" %}Credit ratings{% endgl %} from S&amp;P, Moody's and Fitch sort borrowers into two broad groups. <strong>Investment grade</strong> (BBB− and above) are the safer borrowers. Below that is {% gl "high yield" %}high yield{% endgl %}, also called junk: riskier companies paying higher spreads. The line matters enormously, because many pension funds and insurers may only hold investment grade. When a company is downgraded across it (a "fallen angel", like General Motors in 2005), they have to sell.</p>
    <p>Credit markets often notice trouble before share prices do. A widening spread or a falling bond price can be an early warning for equity investors, which is one reason long/short equity analysts watch credit closely.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

