  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Credit hedge funds differ from ordinary bond funds in four ways: they can borrow to amplify returns, they can bet against companies through {% gl "credit default swap" %}CDS{% endgl %}, they can hold hard-to-sell or defaulted debt that most institutions can't, and they can move from aggressive to defensive as the credit cycle turns.</p>
  </div>
{% endsec %}

{% sec 3, "Three Ways to Run a Credit Fund", "How do credit hedge funds actually invest?", "Three ways to run a credit fund" %}
  <div class="table-wrap">
    <table class="numbers">
      <thead><tr><th>Approach</th><th>How it makes money</th><th>The catch</th></tr></thead>
      <tbody>
        <tr><td>Long-biased credit</td><td>Owns bonds and loans for their interest (the carry), plus gains if spreads tighten. Borrows \(2\) to \(5\) times its capital to make modest yields worthwhile</td><td>Very exposed to credit downturns, when prices fall and leverage multiplies the loss</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Long/short credit</td><td>Owns credits it thinks are undervalued and buys CDS protection on ones it thinks are weakening</td><td>Earns less in strong rallies, in exchange for protection in downturns</td></tr>
        <tr><td>Distressed-for-control ({% gl "loan-to-own" %}loan-to-own{% endgl %})</td><td>Buys enough of a struggling company's debt, at the fulcrum, to steer the bankruptcy and end up owning the company</td><td>Needs huge capital, bankruptcy lawyers, operating skill and years of patience</td></tr>
      </tbody>
    </table>
  </div>
  <div class="prose">
    <p>Loan-to-own blurs into private equity. Its best-known names, Apollo, Oaktree (Howard Marks's firm) and Elliott, all run both kinds of business.</p>
    <p>The long-biased approach has a shape you've met before: steady income most of the time, then a sharp loss when the cycle turns. The interactive shows why leverage is the deciding factor.</p>
  </div>
  <div class="lab">
    <div class="lab-head"><h3>Carry and the credit cycle</h3><p>A fund owns high-yield bonds yielding \(8\%\) (a \(4\%\) government yield plus a \(4\%\) spread) and borrows at \(5\%\) to own more. Pick the kind of period and the leverage, and see where the return comes from. The bonds' prices fall about \(4\%\) for every \(1\) percentage point the spread widens, and defaults lose \(60\%\) of what's owed.</p></div>
